// TEST: Struct Where Paren Form
// ============================================================================
// R2 of the T4 where-ruling: `where (p1, p2)` is an ACCEPTED spelling of the
// canonical comma-separated conjunct list (`where p1, p2`, structs/016).
// It honors the owner's `where(constraints)` parenthesization.
//
// It is NOT a new grammar. parseConjuncts (Parser.fs:2862) runs the full
// expression parser, so `(lo <= mid, mid <= hi)` arrives as ONE conjunct whose
// top node is a literal ExprTuple; Ast.structConjuncts flattens a literal
// tuple/array conjunct into its elements. Zero parser change.
//
// Why the flattening is a STRICT WIDENING (the ruling's decisive argument,
// verified rather than assumed): at declaration EVERY conjunct must unify with
// Bool (TypeCheck.fs:9847, StructWhereNotBool) — structs/031 is that refusal
// firing. A tuple-typed conjunct is therefore a HARD ERROR at HEAD, so
// flattening can only turn errors into acceptances; no accepting program
// changes meaning. R3 keeps it literal-only: a non-literal tuple/array-valued
// conjunct is still the structs/031 error.
//
// This file is the accept twin of structs/016 — same struct, same solution,
// different spelling, so a divergence between the two is a flattening bug.
// ============================================================================
struct Ranged {
    lo: Int,
    hi: Int,
    mid: Int
} where (lo <= mid, mid <= hi)
let r = Ranged { lo = 1, mid = 5, hi = 9 }
let m = r.mid
// EXPECT: m = 5
